  "industry_insights": {
    "automotive": "Across the automotive sector, buyers are consolidating supply around IATF16949-certified factories with full traceability. High-strength grades (10.9 and 12.9) with Dacromet or zinc-nickel finishes are the ones seeing the longest lead times.",
    "machinery": "Machinery and equipment makers are tightening tolerances on standard and non-standard fasteners as production lines get more precise. Custom small-batch parts with short lead times are increasingly hard to source.",
    "construction": "Construction suppliers are paying more attention to corrosion resistance and complete documentation on structural fasteners. Dacromet and zinc finishes with material certificates are becoming the baseline for export markets.",
    "electronics": "Electronics and 3C assembly lines run on small precision fasteners (M1.6 to M6) where consistency matters more than price. Buyers increasingly ask for clean packaging and per-batch dimensional reports.",
    "new_energy": "Solar and new-energy projects are driving demand for stainless and high-corrosion-resistance fasteners that hold up for decades outdoors. Traceable material certificates are now a standard requirement.",
    "medical": "Medical automation and device makers prioritize precision, surface finish, and traceability on every batch of small fasteners. Documentation and consistent quality matter more than price per piece.",
    "hardware_furniture": "Hardware distributors are watching inventory closely and prefer suppliers with broad standard ranges and fast dispatch. A full line card with stable quality lets distributors quote confidently.",
    "general": "Industrial buyers are increasingly qualifying a second factory source for fasteners to protect against supply disruption. Consistent quality documentation and quick response times are what keep suppliers on the approved list."
  }
